==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|extemporise}} {{qualifier|mostly Commonwealth}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[perform]] or [[speak]] without [[prior]] [[plan]]ning or [[thought]]; to act in an [[impromptu]] manner; to [[improvise]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1881|author=w:George MacDonald|title=Mary Marston|chapter=35|passage="Will you please tell me whose music you have been playing?" . . .<br>"It's nobody's, miss."<br>"Do you mean you have been '''extemporizing''' all this time?"}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=March 5 2009|author=[[w:Peter Baker (author)|Peter Baker]]|title=The (very) scripted president|titleurl=http://www.nytimes.com/2009/03/05/world/americas/05iht-prompt.4.20623004.html?pagewanted=all|journal=New York Times|accessdate=8 Nov 2011
|passage=But while some of his predecessors liked to '''extemporize''', Obama prefers the message to be just so.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[adapt]], improvise, or [[devise]] action or speech in an impromptu or [[spontaneous]] manner.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1860|author=w:Nathaniel Hawthorne|title=The Marble Faun|chapter=10|passage=As the music came fresher on their ears, they danced to its cadence, '''extemporizing''' new steps and attitudes.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1879|author=w:Samuel Butler|title=Evolution, Old & New|chapter=5|passage=The small jelly-speck, which we call the amoeba, has no organs save what it can '''extemporize''' as occasion arises.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1906|author=w:Thomas Hardy|title=The Dynasts|section=Part Second, Act Third
|passage=The wine runs into pitchers, washing-basins, shards, chamber- vessels, and other '''extemporized''' receptacles.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=Aug 25 2003|author=Emily Eakin|title=How King Shaped The Dream|titleurl=http://www.nytimes.com/2003/08/25/arts/on-view-how-king-shaped-the-dream.html?pagewanted=all&src=pm|journal=New York Times|accessdate=8 Nov 2011
|passage=His most famous words — "I have a dream" — were '''extemporized'''.}}

